<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
        How many rows the horizontal grids in the editor's panels lay out, bucketed by
        available height on the same three tiers as dimens.xml:

            values/          under 520dp   compact   — landscape, split screen, tiny phones
            values-h520dp/   520 – 639dp   medium    — short and older phones
            values-h640dp/   640dp and up  full      — tall phones and tablets

        These grids scroll sideways, so a row costs height and buys nothing but a shorter
        sideways scroll. On a short screen the panel has barely a hundred dp for its page,
        and four rows of swatches fill that on their own — the border width slider under
        them ends up off the bottom. Fewer rows keeps the whole control visible without
        taking the height out of the canvas.
    -->
    <integer name="panel_color_grid_rows">1</integer>
    <integer name="panel_preset_grid_rows">1</integer>

    <!--
        Rows in the browse strips that MorphGridLayoutManager drives - the resting,
        horizontal half of the panels you pick fonts, styles, objects, images, shapes,
        vectors and tables from. Kept separate from panel_preset_grid_rows because the
        container is a different size entirely: measured on a Pixel 10 Pro XL, the browse
        strip is 185dp tall at h640dp but only 46dp at h520dp, where the preset grid still
        has 99dp. Tuning one should not silently move the other.

        These adapters floor a tile at 24dp, so too many rows do not shrink the tile, they
        overflow the strip and clip it: at 46dp, three rows want 3 * (24 + 6) = 90dp and
        you see the top of row one and nothing else - which is why the small-screen panel
        showed bare download arrows and no previews at all.
    -->
    <integer name="panel_browse_grid_rows">1</integer>
</resources>
